<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jorge Amado</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1912">1912</a>–<a href="/years/2001">2001</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jorge Amado was a Brazilian novelist from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacao">cacao</a> region of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahia">Bahia</a> and one of the most widely read writers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a>. His early novels portrayed the violent struggle for the cacao lands, and later works such as Gabriela, Clove and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cinnamon">Cinnamon</a> (1958) and Dona Flor and Her Two Husbands (1966) celebrated the life of Bahia. He was also a Communist deputy in 1946.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jorge-amado.jpg" alt="Jorge Amado" width="320" height="450" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bernard Gotfryd · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jorge_Amado,_gtfy.00010.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
